# The Direct Solution of the Discrete Poisson Equation on Irregular Regions

\author{B. L. Buzbee, F. W. Dorr, J. A. George, G. H. Golub}

\begin{abstract}
There are several very fast direct methods which can be used to solve the discrete
Poisson equation on rectangular domains. We show that these methods can also be
used to treat problems on irregular regions.
\end{abstract}

## 1. Introduction

Reduces the discrete Poisson problem $Au=y$ on an irregular region to a
sequence of problems $Bz=w$ on a rectangular super-region, where $B$ has the
same structure as $A$ except in $p$ rows corresponding to boundary/interface
points. Fast Poisson solvers (Buneman / Hockney / cyclic reduction; see refs
[6,8,11,16]) then handle each $Bz=w$ in $\Theta(N^2 \log N)$ operations.

## 2. Method when det B != 0  (capacitance matrix)

Given $A = (A_1; A_2)^\top$ with $A_1$ the $p$ replaced rows and $A_2 = B_2$,
form $B = (B_1; A_2)^\top$. Pick nonsingular $W \in \mathbb{R}^{p\times p}$
(the paper uses $W = I$). Define the *capacitance matrix*
$$C \;=\; A_1 B^{-1} W \in \mathbb{R}^{p\times p}.$$
Solve $C\beta = y_1 - A_1 B^{-1} y$, then recover
$$x \;=\; B^{-1}(y + \bar W \beta), \qquad
\bar W = \begin{pmatrix} W \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}.$$
This is the Sherman-Morrison-Woodbury formula applied to the low-rank update
$A = B + FG$ with $F = \bar W$, $G = W^{-1}(A_1 - B_1)$.

## 3. Method when rank(B) = n-1  (Neumann case)

Handles the rank-1 deficient case (Neumann-Neumann embedding). Chooses a
consistency-preserving right-hand side; substitutes a null-vector column into
$C$ so it becomes nonsingular.

## 4. Applications by Imbedding

Rectangle-with-hole (Fig. 1), rotating-fluid annular region (Fig. 2),
plasma z-r geometry (Fig. 3). Embed $R_h \subset \hat R_h$; the boundary
overlap with $\hat R_h$ contributes the $p$ replaced rows.

## 5. Applications by Splitting

Elongated L-shape (Fig. 4) and piecewise-material rectangle (Fig. 5). Split
into two rectangular subproblems joined along a common interface line;
the interface unknowns are the $p$ capacitance-matrix DOFs.

## 6. Computational Results (Table 1)

Two rectangle-with-inner-square regions; unit outer square with a
symmetrically-centered inner square removed. Region 1: inner side 1/2.
Region 2: inner side 1/4. Test function $u(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$ (no truncation
error). CDC 6600. Iterative comparisons: SOR, SLOR, ADI (Peaceman-Rachford
with Wachspress parameters, cycle length 4). Direct method: variant 1 of
Buneman algorithm (Buzbee-Golub-Nielson 1970) on the imbedding rectangle.

